<% layout('./layout') %>
<article class="panel prose legal">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="muted">Every number on a report comes from the rules below. They are deliberately simple, so you can check them yourself.</p>

  <h2>Versions</h2>
  <p>With a <code>package-lock.json</code> (npm 5 or newer), the exact installed version is checked. Without one, the <b>lowest</b> published version your range allows is checked (worst case), and those findings are marked “may already be fixed”.</p>

  <h2>Findings</h2>
  <table class="rules">
    <thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Known vulnerability (OSV.dev)</td><td>The advisory's own severity: <span class="sev critical">Critical</span> <span class="sev high">High</span> <span class="sev medium">Medium</span> <span class="sev low">Low</span><div class="muted small">Without a severity: Critical for a known malicious package, otherwise Medium.</div></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Package deprecated by its authors</td><td><span class="sev high">High</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Only the version you use is deprecated</td><td><span class="sev medium">Medium</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>No release for more than 2 years</td><td><span class="sev medium">Medium</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Single npm maintainer</td><td><span class="sev low">Low</span> <span class="muted small">Medium if also idle for 2 years</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1–2 major versions behind</td><td><span class="sev low">Low</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 or more major versions behind</td><td><span class="sev medium">Medium</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A package's risk is its worst finding. <code>@types/*</code> packages skip the single-maintainer and major-versions-behind findings: DefinitelyTyped publishes all of them under one account, and their major version follows the runtime or library they describe.</p>

  <h2>Health score</h2>
  <p>Start at 100 and subtract per package. devDependencies count half, and the score never goes below 0.</p>
  <div class="score-rules">
    <div><span class="sev critical">Critical</span><b>−25</b></div>
    <div><span class="sev high">High</span><b>−12</b></div>
    <div><span class="sev medium">Medium</span><b>−5</b></div>
    <div><span class="sev low">Low</span><b>−1</b></div>
  </div>
  <p>Maintenance findings (no recent release, a single maintainer, major versions behind) take off at most 30 points in total, so a project whose only issue is quiet dependencies never drops below 70. Vulnerabilities and deprecations always count in full.</p>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>85–100</dt><dd>Healthy</dd>
    <dt>60–84</dt><dd>Needs attention</dd>
    <dt>Below 60</dt><dd>At risk</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Upgrade plan and estimates</h2>
  <p>Steps are ordered by risk removed per hour of work. Estimates are rough ranges:</p>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>Patch or minor upgrade</dt><dd>~15 min</dd>
    <dt>Renamed package</dt><dd>~30 min</dd>
    <dt>Major upgrade</dt><dd>0.5–1 day, plus 0.5 day per extra major</dd>
    <dt>Replace a deprecated package</dt><dd>0.5–1 day</dd>
    <dt>Low-risk maintenance finding</dt><dd>Listed as “monitor”, with no estimate</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Data sources</h2>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>npm registry</dt><dd>Versions, deprecation notices, maintainers, release dates</dd>
    <dt>npm downloads</dt><dd>Weekly download counts</dd>
    <dt>OSV.dev</dt><dd>Known vulnerabilities</dd>
    <dt>deps.dev</dt><dd>Source repository and OpenSSF Scorecard</dd>
  </dl>
  <p class="muted small">Data is cached for 24 hours.</p>
</article>
